courses.learning_outcomes:
Upon completion of this course, students will be able to:

1. acquire knowledge, skills and attitudes related to inter-professional collaboration and practice in health care;
2. acquire knowledge in the health care promotion and disease treatment and prevention;
3. contextualize health care issues in the holistic and interprofessional context; and
4. identify and examine the significant roles and functions of social workers in health care system in Hong Kong.

After completing this online course, students will be able to:

1. Understand the concepts and value of S-L for the society and their personal development;
2. Apply the skills of communicating with various vulnerable communities;
3. Analyze the theories on assessing the needs of communities;
4. Apply the concepts of designing, planning, implementing and evaluating innovative service projects meeting the needs of the targeted communities; and
5. Reflect and integrate S-L with knowledge and skills acquired in their own disciplines.

Upon completion of this course, students will be able to:

1. Acquire knowledge of the key concepts and theoretical perspectives of multiculturalism, related social policies and service delivery system;

2. Apply an intercultural perspective to understand the struggles, challenges and socio-cultural barriers of ethnic groups in Hong Kong.

3. Evaluate the impact of culture on individuals and/or groups’ values, beliefs, behaviours, attitudes, preferences and life pursuits;

4. Develop awareness in cultural sensitivity and acquire cultural competence as well as intercultural intelligence

Upon completion of the course, students are able to:

1. Understand human rights concepts and the international and regional human rights mechanisms;
2. Identify and understand major debates on equality and justice;
3. Critically analyze the impact of globalization on international and local policymaking;
4. Develop a sensitivity and resolution towards understanding conflicting rights situations under the influence of culture, politics, and economic;
5. Understand the interplay between international, regional and local actors in shaping the execution of policy; and

6. Explore the latest trends in international and social movements on global justice and policy advocacy.

The primary aim of the course is to provide students with value, knowledge and skills on employment and labour services round the globe in a comparative approach. By the end of the course, students should be able to:

1. Develop an understanding of the meaning and nature of labour and employment issues in Hong Kong and other contemporary societies;
2. Examine the extent of economic insecurity and unemployment in Hong Kong and in other societies e.g. China and UK;
3. Become familiar with the historical development, political and economic context, current provisions of labour and employment services in Hong Kong;
4. Study and review labour and employment services as a strategy in combat economic insecurity and unemployment;
5. Analyze current debate regarding the philosophy, strategy and future direction of labour and employment services in Hong Kong and in other countries.

Upon completion of the course, students will be able to:

a. understand the needs of people with special needs and their families, especially the psychosocial aspects, educational opportunities and legal aspects;
b. assess the roles of social workers in service provision, with special focus on inter-disciplinary cooperation among different professionals, case management and managed care;
c. critically appraise current services, legal and social context for people with special needs in Hong Kong; and

d. reflect the social construction and cultural perspective of disability.

Upon completion of the course, students will be able to:

1. utilize empirical methods to answer research questions;
2. implement different statistical and analytical methods, such as SPSS;
3. critically evaluate the effectiveness of particular research methods for different target groups;
4. analyze and apply research findings in social work practice;
4. 5. address values and ethical issues when implementing the scientific approach to social work practice; and

6. demonstrate understanding of ethical use of data and empirical information to address human problems.

Upon completion of this course, students will be able to:
1. Recognize significant ethical and legal issues concerning practice in marriage and family therapy;
2. Have enhanced sensitivity to legal and ethical issues in marriage and family therapy;
3. Do no harm to individuals and families when conducting marriage and family therapy;
4. Demonstrate competence to work with individuals, couples and families with diverse demographic and cultural backgrounds;
5. Respect and embrace human diversity; and
6. Have a self-reflection on personal and professional values and is able to adhere to professional values in practicing marriage and family therapy.

Upon the completion of the course, students will be able to:

- utilize systemic and contextual thinking in practice;
- apply the therapeutic and interpersonal skills essential to the success of the marriage and family therapy;
- administer different family assessment instruments appropriately and skillfully;
- work with family and larger systems effectively and collaboratively; and

- adhere to the ethical values and principles of family and couple work through a reflective practice.

Upon the completion of the course, the students are expected to be able to:
1. Appreciate the reciprocal interaction among human cognition, emotion, behavior and environment;
2. Examine human psychosocial problems from a cognitive-behavioral perspective;
3. Familiarize with major modalities of cognitive behavioral therapy and acquire skills in cognitive-behavioral intervention;
4. Be aware of major application issues of CBI;
5. Adapt skills and techniques of cognitive-behavioral intervention in the local context of social work practice.

Upon completion of the course, students are able to:

1. Appreciate how the family organize under the family systems perspective;
2. Be aware of the paradigmatic shift from modernism to postmodernism in family therapy and its implications to solution-focused approach;
3. Understand the basic assumptions and theoretical foundation of solution-focused therapy;
4. Acquire knowledge and skills in solution-focused therapy;
5. Make differential application of solution-focused therapy in the cultural context of Hong Kong; and

6. Develop a personalized approach to therapy.

By the end of the course, students are able to
1. acquire an extensive understanding of the theories and approaches in systems thinking;
2. become familiar with the major marriage and family therapy approaches;
3. grasp the values and principles of marriage and family therapy;
4. become equipped with knowledge and skills in couple/family assessment and couple/family intervention; and

5. develop the ability to apply marriage and family therapy in different social work settings and with various target populations.

After completing this course, students should be able to:

1. Integrate values, knowledge and skills of social work practice;
2. Analyse the needs and the characteristics of the targeted clienteles they serve;
3. Understand the interactions between client system and the social environment;
4. Formulate appropriate goals and approaches in meeting the needs of the clienteles and/or solving their problems;
5. Implement intervention strategies and skills in the helping process; and
6. Design effective methodology in the evaluation of their direct practice.

Upon successful completion of the course, students will be able to:

1. understand the changing concepts of risk and resilience;

2. become familiar with the existential-phenomenological and hermeneutical-poststructuralist approaches on the main issues of youth work and the target groups to be served;

3. explore the life concerns and stories of at-risk youth and know how this exploration can help guide their intervention goals and actions;

4. equip themselves with practical skills in nurturing resilience in at-risk youth;

5. integrate the existential-phenomenological and hermeneutical-poststructuralist approaches with other youth work models; and

6. develop effective ways in strengthening family resilience and fostering resilience in school and community.

Upon completion of the course, students will be able to:

1) apply various theoretical perspectives in analysing the challenges faced by the children and youth with special needs;
2) apply knowledge of various intervention approaches to facilitate the growth and personal adjustment of children and youth with special needs at the family and school context;
3) develop collaborative methods to work effectively with various professional teams in helping children and youth with special needs;
4) examine critically current education, health and social policies related to children and youth with special needs; and
5) mobilize effective strategies for the advocacy of a more enabling social environment for the children and youth with special needs.

Upon completion of the course, students will be able to:
1) understand the underlying assumptions, theoretical concepts and helping principles of family-centered practice;
2) acquire the knowledge and skills of family-centered practice in working with children and youth and for clients suffering from mental health problems;
3) develop a culturally specific and socially relevant family-centered practice models for families with children and youth in needs and members suffering from mental health problems;
4) critically examine the roles and functions of social workers in relation to other helping professions in the process of family-centered practice.

* *The primary aim of the course is to provide students with value, knowledge and skills on social work intervention in the urban Cities in China. After completing this course, students should be able to:**

1. Locate the development of social work in China within the process of “Globalization” and “Urbanization”;
2. Appreciate the importance of values in social work intervention: universal vs. cultural specific;
3. Categorize various models of social services in urban China; and distinguish different roles of NGOs and Government in these models
4. Examine the characteristics of social work intervention in contemporary urban China;
5. Evaluate the strength and weakness of different social work services;
6. Plan a social work intervention project for a specific target groups in urban China.

Upon completion of the course, students will be able to:
1) understand the underlying assumptions, theoretical concepts and helping principles of family-centered practice;
2) acquire the knowledge and skills of family-centered practice in working with children and youth and for clients suffering from mental health problems;
3) develop a culturally specific and socially relevant family-centered practice models for families with children and youth in needs and members suffering from mental health problems;
4) critically examine the roles and functions of social workers in relation to other helping professions in the process of family-centered practice.

Upon completion of the course, students will be able to:

1. appreciate the importance of evidence-based clinical practice in reviewing different intervention approaches for families with children and youth suffering from mental health problems and/or mental disorders;

2. acquire the bio-psycho-social perspectives in conceptualizing children and youth with mental health needs;

3. be equipped with knowledge and skills of family-centered care for children and youth with mental health needs; and

4. develop their model of practice, which is contextually based, for families with children and youth suffering from mental health problems and/or mental disorders.

Upon completion of the course, students will be able to:

1. appreciate the importance of evidence-based clinical practice in reviewing different intervention approaches for adults suffering from mental health problems and/or mental disorders;
2. acquire bio-psycho-social perspectives in conceptualizing adults with mental health needs;
3. equip themselves with knowledge and skills of family-centered care for adults with mental health needs; and
4. develop a model of practice that is contextually based for mental health services.

Upon completion of the course, students will be able to:

1) integrate their learning from various courses of the program;

2) integrate their knowledge and skills of social work research in need assessment, service planning, program evaluation or policy formulation;

3) integrate the micro and macro perspectives in understanding the needs of the specific target group and in formulating their service approach or policy alternative; and

4) formulate culturally specific, holistic, and innovative service approaches or policy alternatives according to the needs of the specific target group and the context in which these needs arise.
